#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e6dd7dd-a9af-5b67-8bc5-b3e3616d5b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459687} {:answer/id #uuid "875c6a37-c169-53f0-bcaf-d71179240c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "875c6a37-c169-53f0-bcaf-d71179240c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459688} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bc99ce9-0abe-5b12-a906-54691a59cac2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460710} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ebff733-5e48-5f0d-8c7d-7d87c5b28ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459689} {:answer/id #uuid "954c6b9e-d5a5-5abe-a691-9554db80b606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ebff733-5e48-5f0d-8c7d-7d87c5b28ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459689} :answer-field/id #uuid "48eeff13-aa1f-5016-983a-eb5eff08fa76" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460711} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00634cba-72e7-5c17-a0da-5f682bf47501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459691} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8ac5ea-9483-54c7-866a-2654e9f34e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb8ac5ea-9483-54c7-866a-2654e9f34e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459692} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c08c6f8-83b9-5897-a1d0-d8e61df77b44" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9482778-6f45-5e93-913d-289acb526444" :question/math-academy-id "q-181536" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-5n+6>0}$\n\n- **II.** $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-5m+6<0}$\n\n- **III.** $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-5q+6<0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x+6$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/0a/0a42815f626652c332862a38a3812463a9407605329bd81df25f11274b26ce6f.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<2\\,\\text{or}\\,x>3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n2<x<3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(2,3).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-5n+6>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,0,1}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-5m+6<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-5q+6<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18dee266-98ce-54c1-aeac-12e36d53c9f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459693} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfad166-e1d5-5576-97fc-d582dfcd13b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18dee266-98ce-54c1-aeac-12e36d53c9f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459693} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd83230-91f8-5fa6-922e-bf8570620d34" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460713} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "382ef46e-a95e-5d40-a99d-679cb662d3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459695} {:answer/id #uuid "eab8131c-ba52-5510-9ce0-7a446f777dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eab8131c-ba52-5510-9ce0-7a446f777dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459696} :answer-field/id #uuid "14b4259e-8704-56d4-8467-5956a49e9711" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21169332-e448-5bc4-82aa-9564cff51482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459697} {:answer/id #uuid "5547c0e5-4726-5d07-969c-a5ef5603969d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5547c0e5-4726-5d07-969c-a5ef5603969d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459698} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b949af-bf05-5158-8cb8-5296b286a6e6" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e83b937-f1ab-5064-ab2b-f81fab22743d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181597" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n- **A.** $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi )=1}$\n- **B.** $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2q\\pi )=1}$\n- **C.** $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\pi )=1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\pi )=1$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}+m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{1}{4}+m\\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB={…,-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{9}{4},…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8f53bd8-9ef9-57f4-8a63-cec3891f5e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459699} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffae41e-b8f8-54f4-bbf2-3f0a2cc9c3a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8f53bd8-9ef9-57f4-8a63-cec3891f5e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459699} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a2266ca-93a8-5492-80b5-97a981aaf710" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b29a5306-4144-5f79-908a-f8feff4ba33a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459701} {:answer/id #uuid "03e9eb1b-02d8-506f-82f1-512dd85f1977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03e9eb1b-02d8-506f-82f1-512dd85f1977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459702} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cfa2ff5-23d6-55fb-acac-2e57ae1265d5" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460717} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad9d171-6dd3-541c-afb8-e1779bf6036a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459703} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e2da5c-32be-56a9-8008-4e08536eb809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7e2da5c-32be-56a9-8008-4e08536eb809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459704} :answer-field/id #uuid "a85a6640-66d8-5a27-8abe-3ec0ab8d48ff" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e02e6bd4-a148-57c6-a612-60228a140b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181599" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n- **A.** $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({x}^{2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- **B.** $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- **C.** $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)=2 \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>3,$ we have ${f}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nA={{x}^{2},2x,2,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\frac{1}{x},-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})=-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}})=\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\sqrt{x},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8709e44f-9eb1-5aa6-bcfe-43cf3fc3faf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462211} {:answer/id #uuid "27d5ab67-21dd-5ddf-963d-a807ba7fa6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27d5ab67-21dd-5ddf-963d-a807ba7fa6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462212} :answer-field/id #uuid "36cba5d5-c85e-5904-a5c8-298686c30fc8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462859} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e8c7c96-b1cc-5dde-a9a0-abbbbc82ee3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462213} {:answer/id #uuid "026c8874-89cb-59ed-b68e-4121b69a008f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "026c8874-89cb-59ed-b68e-4121b69a008f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462214} :answer-field/id #uuid "908ef17b-a642-5b4a-a3a3-f8a1c602221f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462860} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ae0ddd9-724e-56a2-bcab-8344c45a63ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462215} {:answer/id #uuid "dee8c65a-8131-573c-a2b7-3b5c4032f90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dee8c65a-8131-573c-a2b7-3b5c4032f90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462216} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6d889c-fee7-5bfa-bcf5-7ab3278080c2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16d5f3be-e7a0-5c29-a089-f858318b66bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-181722" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|2n|>5}$\n\n- **II.** $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|2q|>5}$\n\n- **III.** $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|2x|>5}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x|>5 \\\\ 2|x|>5 \\\\ |x|>\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})\\cup (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty ).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|2n|>5$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {3,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|2q|>5$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c26aeaa-9252-5bd3-9ad6-0fc25ca6b03c" :knowledge-point/title "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f3fd87-f83d-5f24-9a9c-2f1e10b494f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462217} {:answer/id #uuid "49c73b7a-7a95-579d-9ed4-ed7f399c4ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49c73b7a-7a95-579d-9ed4-ed7f399c4ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462218} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff59416c-f6a2-5db0-b15e-fdd93adea941" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e26dd87-222d-53c2-b445-02b6c273d202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462219} {:answer/id #uuid "99c008c3-91fd-5472-8d4c-0c81d6a0763e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99c008c3-91fd-5472-8d4c-0c81d6a0763e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462220} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff83462-5be9-5b17-9714-db25c409d85a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462863} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "647e34ca-f77c-50e8-873d-b3b07431819c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462221} {:answer/id #uuid "0c51f730-21dd-5e1c-815e-a111ba00fff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "647e34ca-f77c-50e8-873d-b3b07431819c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462221} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30794bc-281a-5d41-943b-b8c998897fa2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462864} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b92dd1c2-7470-51b7-84bd-43a97469e0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462223} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8c081b-8a77-5970-9a81-d525e92f384c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d8c081b-8a77-5970-9a81-d525e92f384c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462224} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa0501cc-b8f8-5bbe-b3f3-b801954fc967" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "587a7a92-f47b-5043-840d-bba7363fe179" :question/math-academy-id "q-183495" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $6\\,|\\,x$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $3\\,|\\,x$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n- **I.** $6\\,|\\,x$ is sufficient for $3\\,|\\,x$\n- **II.** For $3\\,|\\,x,$ it is sufficient that $6\\,|\\,x$\n- **III.** $6\\,|\\,x$ is necessary for $3\\,|\\,x$\n- **IV.** For $6\\,|\\,x,$ it is necessary that $3\\,|\\,x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is sufficient for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{it is sufficient that}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is necessary for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option IV reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{it is necessary that}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b91f3c08-8c48-5112-9ed4-298a23417e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462225} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb48aee-165f-553f-943a-1dc1a93a1ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eb48aee-165f-553f-943a-1dc1a93a1ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462226} :answer-field/id #uuid "95d9122a-4241-5d15-911c-ee537ed4c9d2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462866} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8bea4de-24a6-51d3-9d13-9aec0ac73d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462227} {:answer/id #uuid "215cb620-ff23-54df-9c49-5769271739ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8bea4de-24a6-51d3-9d13-9aec0ac73d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462227} :answer-field/id #uuid "a424a42b-0971-57df-b20a-9729c34079ef" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462867} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b97ac9-352c-5fb4-8ec4-f180d08d2c48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462229} {:answer/id #uuid "247d398a-1b3a-5517-a755-c5805f82cc29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "247d398a-1b3a-5517-a755-c5805f82cc29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462230} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c38dc07-5d66-5619-a22f-f60f5317be94" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e5c1c70-9337-53c9-94bd-f7f57b8f63c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-212112" :question/problem "![](images/01/015cb5b350ad2692c1371d20c367e6c01a81b981351ba33b61e6c95854862d63.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the sets $X=\\{2,3,4\\}$ and $Y=\\{♣,♠\\},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,♠)$\n\nII. $(3,♣)$\n\nIII. $(4,♣)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,♠)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(3,♣)$ does not belong to the relation since the plot does not contain a point with such coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(4,♣)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf7c76b-8b6e-5119-a86f-3