 "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461435} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f0ca48-44d1-5bd6-9830-c490f17a2d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7edd4edd-c2bd-595e-87cd-340b998892bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461435} :answer-field/id #uuid "38dd7821-f0f2-5810-bd0f-71890d0ec23b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe40ba69-5c80-5288-8a8c-e5c1e0d1d354" :question/math-academy-id "q-105419" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c75313fbb97784b27ea0c57068e7221e0f9416ac4857b70723fd7ca949d9aba7.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)$\n- $x\\in [-3,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-3].$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and also left-continuous at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,0)$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-3,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(-3,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :